cowsay-elks 1.1
===============
A small native C adaptation of the classic cowsay for ELKS on 8086/8088.
It does not need Perl. The cow and formatting conventions come from
Tony Monroe's cowsay 3.03 (copyright 1999-2000 Tony Monroe):
https://github.com/tnalpgge/rank-amateur-cowsay
Distributed under GPL-3.0-or-later; see LICENSE. No warranty.
The C implementation is newly written, not the full upstream Perl program.

Install
-------
Copy bin/cowsay, bin/cowthink, bin/elksay, and bin/elkthink to /bin
on ELKS, then:
  chmod 755 /bin/cowsay /bin/cowthink /bin/elksay /bin/elkthink

Examples
--------
  elksay Hello ELKS
  elkthink Thinking about lunch
  cowsay -f elk Hello ELKS
  cowsay Hello ELKS
  echo Hello from stdin | cowsay
  fortune | cowsay
  cowsay -W 30 -b An 8088 can moo too
  cowsay -e ^^ -T vv Hello
  cowthink -d Thinking about lunch
  cowsay -n < message.txt

Options
-------
-b borg; -d dead; -g greedy; -p paranoid; -s stoned; -t tired;
-w wired; -y young. These may be grouped, e.g. -bd.
-e eyes and -T tongue accept up to two printable ASCII characters.
-W width sets the maximum message columns (1..255; default 40).
-n preserves lines, expands tabs at eight-column stops and removes CR.
-f default or -f default.cow selects the built-in cow.
-f elk or -f elk.cow selects the built-in elk.
Invoking as elksay or elkthink selects the elk by default.
-l lists available cows; -h or --help gives help; --version gives version.
-- ends option parsing. Remaining arguments are joined with spaces.
No arguments reads stdin. The executable name cowthink selects thoughts.

Limits
------
ASCII only. Input is limited to 8191 bytes and 1024 output lines. Tab
expansion is also limited to 8191 bytes. With -n, each line is limited to
255 columns. Oversized or non-ASCII input is rejected, not silently cut.
The default cow and an original ASCII elk are built in: external Perl
.cow files and COWPATH
are not supported. Wrapping is simple whitespace-based wrapping, with
long words split at the requested width, rather than Perl Text::Wrap's
exact behavior. This is a compact adaptation, not full upstream parity.
Select a width of 76 or less for an 80-column screen (bubble adds 4).

Build
-----
An ELKS libc/toolchain is required; set these paths for your checkout:
  make ELKS_ROOT=/path/to/elks TOOLCHAIN=/path/to/toolchain
The build targets i8086, small model, ELKS libc. For host-side CLI checks:
  make host
  python3 test_cli.py
